From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 01:38:32 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Embedding: an instruction that faults has changed nothing

The embedding host resolves a guest page fault, a copy-on-write store
among them, and runs the faulting instruction again from the state the
fault left, so that state must be the one the instruction found. Some
instructions changed state before their last access could fault:

- A read-modify-write wrote the flags before its store found the page
  read-only, so ADC, SBB, RCL and RCR ran again with the carry they
  had computed. A pass before register allocation now finds each load
  whose bytes a store of the same instruction writes, and the backend
  translates it with EMBEDDER_ACCESS_READ_WRITE, so the write fault,
  or the end of a unit that stores into its own code, comes at the
  load.
- POP to memory updated RSP before its store. The store now comes
  first, its address formed from a node of its own. PUSHA, a far CALL
  and ENTER store from the old stack pointer before they change it,
  a 16-bit ENTER changing BP alone, and POPA probes its whole frame
  first.
- FLDENV, FRSTOR, FXRSTOR, XRSTOR and IRET loaded state from the first
  part of their operand before a later part faulted, and FLDENV did
  not read all of it. A new EmbedderProbe op faults as a load of the
  whole operand would, and each of them probes its operand first.
- Register allocation coalesced a value with the static register a
  later store wrote, so the register took the value where it was
  computed, before an access between there and the store could fault:
  a constant shared with an earlier instruction's address changed AF
  before that instruction's load faulted. Embedded, a value is not
  coalesced when an operation that can leave the block, a guest
  access or a divide that raises #DE, lies between it and its store.
